---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const schema = `{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "AboutPage",
      "@id": "https://stlaccess.com/about#webpage",
      "url": "https://stlaccess.com/about",
      "name": "About STLACCESS and kitsorix",
      "description": "The mission and mathematical methodology behind the STLACCESS scale conversion engine.",
      "breadcrumb": {
        "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
        "itemListElement": [
          { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://stlaccess.com/" },
          { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "About", "item": "https://stlaccess.com/about" }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Person",
      "@id": "https://stlaccess.com/#person",
      "name": "kitsorix",
      "url": "https://github.com/kitsorix-official",
      "jobTitle": "Lead Developer & 3D Printing Specialist",
      "knowsAbout": ["3D Printing", "Tabletop Gaming", "Scale Modeling", "Mathematics"],
      "sameAs": [
        "https://github.com/kitsorix-official"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "Organization",
      "@id": "https://stlaccess.com/#organization",
      "name": "STLACCESS",
      "url": "https://stlaccess.com/",
      "logo": {
        "@type": "ImageObject",
        "url": "https://stlaccess.com/favicon.svg"
      },
      "founder": { "@id": "https://stlaccess.com/#person" }
    }
  ]
}`;
---

<Layout 
	title="Our Story & Methodology | STLACCESS - Precision 3D Printing Scales"
	description="Meet kitsorix, the creator of STLACCESS. Learn how we solve Scale Creep in tabletop gaming using the 175cm human baseline and high-end 3D printing precision."
	schema={schema}
>
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-20 w-full">
        <!-- Breadcrumbs -->
        <nav class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 pt-12 pb-4 text-[10px] font-black uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-slate-600">
            <a href="/" class="hover:text-[#ff6b35]">Home</a> 
            <span class="mx-2 text-slate-800">/</span> 
            <span class="text-slate-400">About & Methodology</span>
        </nav>

        <!-- Hero Section -->
        <div class="mb-20 text-center">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-[#ff6b35] mb-4 block">The Mission</span>
            <h1 class="text-5xl md:text-7xl font-bold font-heading mb-6 tracking-tight">Precision in a World of <span class="text-[#ff6b35]">Scale Creep</span></h1>
            <p class="text-xl text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">How a 3D printing hobbyist turned mathematical frustration into a free tool for creators to use and learn from.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Story Content -->
        <article class="prose prose-invert max-w-none space-y-12 text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            
            <div class="space-y-6">
                <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white font-heading">The Problem: "32mm" Means Nothing</h2>
                <p>
                    If you've been in the 3D printing or tabletop hobby for more than a week, you've seen it. You download a beautiful "32mm" miniature, print it, and find out it towers over your "28mm" army like a giant. Or worse, it looks like a child standing next to a tank that was supposedly "1:56 scale."
                </p>
                <p>
                    In the industry, we call this <strong>Scale Creep</strong>. Every sculptor has their own definition of height. Some measure to the top of the head, others to the eye level. Some account for base thickness, others don't. For the hobbyist trying to build a cohesive collection, it's a nightmare of wasted resin and failed prints.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-slate-900/40 border border-white/5 p-8 md:p-12 rounded-[2.5rem] glow-box">
                <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white font-heading mb-6">The Solution: The 175cm Baseline</h2>
                <p class="mb-6">
                    STLACCESS was born in a small bedroom workshop. As a 3D printing hobbyist, I realized that while "28mm" is a marketing term, "1:56" is a mathematical ratio. To bridge the gap, I needed a universal truth.
                </p>
                <p>
                    I established the <strong>175cm Human Baseline</strong>. By assuming a standard human height of 1.75 meters, I can calculate exactly how large a model should be in any ratio or system scale. 
                </p>
                <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mt-10">
                    <div class="bg-black/40 p-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/5">
                        <h3 class="text-[#ff6b35] font-bold mb-2">Eye Level (160cm)</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm">The standard for fantasy and sci-fi miniatures where heroic proportions often distort the "top of head" measurement.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-black/40 p-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/5">
                        <h3 class="text-[#ff6b35] font-bold mb-2">Top of Head (175cm)</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm">The gold standard for historical military kits and display-grade busts where realism is paramount.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="space-y-6">
                <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white font-heading">From €40 to High-End Precision</h2>
                <p>
                    My journey started in my bedroom with a tiny <strong>Easythreed</strong> printer from China—a €40 entry point into a world I didn't yet realize I'd be obsessed with. It was loud, it was temperamental, and it taught me everything about the importance of precision the hard way.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Fast forward to today, and my "workshop" has evolved. I now rely on a <strong>Bambu Lab X1 Carbon</strong> with a multi-color filament storage and feed system. This leap in technology—from a budget starter to a high-speed, multi-material powerhouse—is what drives the accuracy of STLACCESS. I've seen the struggle of budget machines and the complexity of high-end ones, and I've built this tool to serve both.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="space-y-6">
                <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white font-heading">Mathematical Certainty for Every Maker</h2>
                <p>
                    My goal is to grow STLACCESS, with the help of the 3D printing community, into the go-to resource for math conversions in tabletop games like Warhammer and more. My engine doesn't just guess; it computes based on our <a href="/reference" class="text-white border-b border-white/20 hover:text-[#ff6b35] transition-colors">verified reference library</a>. I provide the exact percentages you need to enter into your slicer—whether it's Lychee, Chitubox, or Bambu Studio—to ensure that every print fits perfectly within your world.
                </p>
                <p>
                    I believe that scale should be a choice, not a surprise. Precision is the standard, and at STLACCESS, I'm making it accessible to everyone. For technical details on how I maintain global standards, view my <a href="/legal" class="text-white border-b border-white/20 hover:text-[#ff6b35] transition-colors">compliance statement</a>.
                </p>
            </div>

        </article>

        <!-- CTA -->
        <div class="mt-24 pt-12 border-t border-white/5 text-center">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading mb-6">Ready to stop guessing?</h3>
            <a href="/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-3 bg-[#ff6b35] text-black px-10 py-5 rounded-2xl font-bold text-sm uppercase tracking-widest hover:scale-105 transition-transform shadow-xl shadow-[#ff6b35]/20">
                <i class="fa-solid fa-calculator"></i>
                Start Converting Now
            </a>
        </div>
    </div>
</Layout>
